From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Mon, 4 Apr 94 22:19:59 WET DST
Subject: [#94-4-40] The Trouble With Left Anarchists

davisd@nimitz.ee.washington.edu writes:
> I wish people to be able to control private parcels of land, and to
> have their right of control protected. I want them to be able to buy
> and sell land, including the improvements thereon. I just think there
> should be some other reason besides the threat of force for the
> unlanded to respect the property claims of the landed. I would think
> that if all involved were reasonable, the landed would compensate the
> unlanded for their right to control land, since they can demonstrate
> no other reason besides force why the unlanded should agree to the
> situation.

  This system already exists, it's called property tax and extortion.
How would you calculate this "unlanded compensation"? The unlanded
have nothing to bargin with except their coercive power. Whatever
they can extort from property owners will be the price they demand.
How would unlanded compensation be distributed to a global group
(the unlanded) without a state? Why should someone 1000 miles away
from your property get compensation when he might never step foot
on it in his entire life? If the answer is "he shouldn't" we are
left with the intractable problem of deciding who and who isn't
in "range" of your property and possibility harmed by being restricted
from setting foot on it.

   Besides, the majority of the unlanded already derive major
benefits from land owners in the form of production from that
land (it is unlikely that a non-ownership anarchy walk-on-land-anywhere
-when-you-want would lead to the productive efficiency of a capitalist
system) and the land owner's own work in protecting and servicing
the land.

  I would argue that land owners already pay this "unlanded compensation"
in the form of regulations, taxes, vandalism, and police $$$.

> Discussion of this issue is kind of interesting, though. Once it is
> agreed that something is property, there is little left to discuss.
> Whether something should be treated as property, and precisely how,
> reveals much more of interest about the discussion participants.

  I was surprised by my education in Microeconomics because I assumed
that economists were always in favor of state control. When I had
had learned that my Microeconomics text and professor supported
privatization of commons for environmental goals, I was delighted.
The economic benefits of a property system have been well
established and accepted even among liberal economists.

-Ray
