From: grigsby@agames.com (JUMP IN THE FIRE)
Date: Tue, 5 Apr 1994 01:42:26 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-4-46] ECO: Tim's Specious Arguments

This messge is basically another dissection of Tim's specious methods
of argument, with little informational content.  Feel free to skip unless
you want to familiarize yourself with another technique of diversion,
namely "So?", and refamiliarize yourself with the old favorites;
diversion via hairsplitting, random inconsistency, and strawman.

> Of course not.  We weren't talking about whether it was justified, only
> about what the effect of car exhaust is upon public health.  My point was
> that you committed a non-sequitur in blaming the diminished lung capacity
> upon the car exhaust alone, which you most certainly did.

I did not say that.  I probably should have been more clear that,
given the evidence, I view it as a major contributing factor but not
the only one.  I bring it up because it is easier for us, as
individuals, to make transportation choices than to change someone
else's manufacturing methods.

And damn if this strawman isn't the centerpiece of your argument.  

> I have no idea
> what the cause really is; it's up to you to bear the burden of proof if you
> want to blame cars.

Obtuseness is not an argument, Tim: I went through this already.  Here's the
logic.

1) Some quality of the air in the LA basin causes lung damage.
2) Cars (internal-combustion) are a major contributor to pollution 
   in the LA basin.
3) The effects on people of certain contaminants in car exhaust are
   consistent with the observed effects in 1).

Therefore, reducing the amount and/or usage of IC vehicles in 2) should
reduce 1).

The measurements you kindly provided some time ago which show
pollution declining starting in the late 70's (just after the main
air-quality legislation was passed) support this logic.

> But if the basin itself is a co-factor, then this
> would tend to exculpate car exhaust alone where there aren't any such moun-
> tains from your blanket indictment that car exhaust injures public health.

You are being purposely obtuse again.  The location of car exhaust
does not cause it to become intrinsically less harmful.  If it spreads
over a wider area, it simply contaminates more air, land, and water
more slowly.

> I don't want you to feel slighted, but none of the points you make do
> anything to relieve you of the burden of proving that car exhaust causes
> the diminished lung capacity you want to blame on it. 

I refuse to engage in further dialogue with you if you are
going to demand absolute certainty, which it appears that you are.
Would it help if I had a "Dr." in front of my name, or something?

> They are all true,
> but it would be another non-sequitur to conclude from them alone that
> car pollution is to blame.

Any further verbiage from you on this subject must address
the above syllogism.

> >I think if Tim is going to challenge the assumption that the 
> >products of internal combustion are not harmful, it is up to him to 
> >show where the demonstrated harm is coming from.

I should have said "_are_ harmful."  Good thing Tim knew what I meant.

> Nope.  This is silly.  It's like saying: "If Grigsby is going to
> challenge the assumption that he assasinated (sic) President Kennedy, it's
> up to him to show who did."

Your analogy is faulty; you are assuming that only one cause can exist
for the harm (ex. killing Kennedy).

Fortunately, this assumption is not silly if we admit possible
multiple causes: evidence exists that Oswald fired at least some of
the shots, much like evidence exists that IC products are harmful and
cause at least some of the harm.

> This view indicates that blaming something
> is a higher priority for some environmentalists than blaming the right
> thing.

No, it is a result of my syllogism above.  Look at it again if you're
confused.  And you have the gall to accuse _me_ of non-sequiturs...!

Your argument is not that we're blaming the wrong thing; your argument
(to use the analogy) is that Kennedy isn't dead, and anyway Oswald didn't
kill him.  I have addressed this form of self-cancelling proposition
before, and do not feel a need to revisit it.

Logical consistency does not seem to be necessary for your own
arguments, hmm?

I have laid out my case: your only argument has been, "Well, that may
be true, but I'm still not convinced."

	I believe (given your examples) that you are selectively
demanding higher standards of proof from beliefs you have already
chosen based on a pre-selected ideology, namely industrial
libertarianism.  Therefore, I will not engage in argument with you any
more.  Trying to talk with you is like talking to a pull-toy.

	Alternatively, next time you post, I may use your techniques
of "argument" -- if I'm really bored.

	I will, however, still look at any statements you have made that
may approach usable data.

> If you've got better things to do than spend hours looking for citations,
> then, by all means, do them.  Don't waste your time.  If you want to make
> the case for the harmfulness of car exhaust, however, then that might be
> what it takes.

Maybe I just don't want to spend hours convincing someone with a 
deliberate agenda to be obtuse.  The only useful information you have
given me is entirely by accident -- excellent examples of comon techniques
of specious argument.

> You haven't done it yet.

You can repeat it as many times as you want but that won't make it true.

> >Someone as (putatively) concerned with logical thinking as Tim should
> >be able to see that this analogy is invalid.  I can choose not to buy
> >Madonna albums, watch her perform, or read about her; I can't choose
> >to breathe air.
> 
> Wrong on both counts.  I do my best to avoid Madonna, without perfect
> success.  A judgement in my favor in a suit against her for aesthetic torts
> would make this easier for me.  Further, you can choose to breathe air or
> not, by such things as gas masks, oxygen tanks& tents, pressure suits, etc.

Bing!  I think this discussion is over.  Tim's statement stands on
its own.

(Besides, it's still air, and had to come from someplace.  You seem to
enjoy making corrections like this the centerpiece of your argument.
I don't.)

> >Also, I don't think legislative action is the best answer,
> 
> Who's talking about legislative action?  I've been talking about judicial
> action.

Both enforced by the state.

> P.S. Grigsby's bit about how the Antarctic ozone hole has also been proven to
> exist is yet another non-sequitur.  Yes, it exists, but there is once again
> no proof that it is caused by CFCs.  It may be increased by CFCs, 

"I have no proof that cancer is caused by UV light.  Its incidence may
be [is] increased by UV light, ..."  This isn't debate, this is
hairsplitting, which is another of your standard techniques of
argument.

> essentially a natural occurrence caused by the sunless, cold Antarctic 
> winter.
> And since Stuart Card wants references up the wazoo, try the book "Global
> Climate Change," edited by Fred Singer.

I'll look at it.

